# `scripts/` — Extraction & SQL Test Runner

Two scripts. `extract.py` is the entire bronze layer — it's what
populates Postgres from Mongo in the first place. `sql_test.py` is the
generic runner behind every `*_sql_tests` pipeline stage documented in
`tests.md`.

> **Naming note:** `extract.py`'s own module docstring header reads
> `scripts/mongo_exp.py`, and it names itself `"mongo_exp"` internally —
> the logger (`get_logger("mongo_exp")`) and the Spark session
> (`appName("mongo_exp")`) both use that name. The file on disk, and every
> place that invokes it (`run_pipeline.ps1`, the Airflow DAG), calls it
> `extract.py`. Cosmetic only — doesn't affect behavior — but worth
> knowing if you go looking for `"mongo_exp"` in a log file and wonder
> where it came from.

---

## 1. `extract.py` — incremental MongoDB → PostgreSQL extraction

### What it does, in one pass
Auto-discovers every user collection in Mongo (no hardcoded list),
figures out per-collection whether it can load incrementally or needs a
full reload, pulls only the new/changed rows via a pushdown filter,
merges them into Postgres with a real upsert (not append-only), verifies
the result independently, and prints a full Rich report — all driven by
PySpark and the MongoDB Spark Connector.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["parse_args()<br/>--tables --full-refresh --dry-run --watermark-column"] --> B["get_mongo_db()<br/>create Postgres engine directly"]
    B --> C["ensure_control_tables()<br/>etl_watermarks, etl_logs"]
    C --> D{"--tables given?"}
    D -- yes --> E["use that list"]
    D -- no --> F["discover_collections()<br/>skip system.* collections"]
    E --> G["build_spark_session()<br/>check_connector_compatibility() first"]
    F --> G
    G --> H["for each collection:<br/>process_collection(...)"]
    H --> I["spark.stop()"]
    I --> J["render_report()<br/>Rich tables + summary panel"]
    J --> K{"any FAILED /<br/>VALIDATION FAILED?"}
    K -- yes --> L["exit 1"]
    K -- no --> M["exit 0"]
```

### Per-collection decision flow

This is the core logic, run once per collection inside
`process_collection()`:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["sample = find_one()<br/>detect_incremental_column()"] --> B{"--full-refresh?"}
    B -- yes --> C["since = None, mode = full<br/>TRUNCATE existing table first"]
    B -- no --> D{"watermark column found?<br/>(updated_timestamp > updated_at ><br/>created_timestamp > created_at)"}
    D -- yes --> E["since = get_watermark()<br/>mode = incremental"]
    D -- no --> F["since = None, mode = full<br/>(warns: no watermark column)"]
    C --> G["read_collection()<br/>Mongo aggregation $gt pushdown if since set"]
    E --> G
    F --> G
    G --> H["sanitize_for_postgres()<br/>_id -> string, nested -> JSON string"]
    H --> I{"batch_rows == 0?"}
    I -- yes --> J["SKIPPED — still re-validates<br/>and records the watermark run"]
    I -- no --> K{"--dry-run?"}
    K -- yes --> L["DRY-RUN — nothing written"]
    K -- no --> M{"table exists yet?"}
    M -- no --> N["write_direct(): JDBC append<br/>creates the table, all rows inserted"]
    M -- yes --> O{"has an _id column?"}
    O -- no --> P["append-only (no merge key available)"]
    O -- yes --> Q{"unique index on<br/>_id ensured?"}
    Q -- no --> R["falls back to append-only<br/>(logs a warning why)"]
    Q -- yes --> S["staging table + real MERGE<br/>(INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE)"]
    N --> T["validate_collection():<br/>independent re-count vs expected"]
    P --> T
    R --> T
    S --> T
    T --> U{"validation PASS?"}
    U -- yes --> V["upsert_watermark(), status = OK"]
    U -- no --> W["status = VALIDATION FAILED"]
```

### Watermark selection — and a subtle Mongo gotcha

The incremental column is auto-detected per collection, checked in this
priority order against a sample document:
```
updated_timestamp → updated_at → created_timestamp → created_at
```
`updated_*` is preferred over `created_*` deliberately — a document that
changes after insert (an order's status flipping to `Cancelled`, say)
needs to be re-synced, not just newly inserted ones. A collection with
none of these four always does a full reload every run — there's no
watermark to track incrementally.

**The gotcha, straight from the code's own comment:** these watermark
fields are stored in Mongo as plain strings (`"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS"`),
not native BSON dates — and MongoDB never matches across BSON types in a
comparison, so a `$gt` filter has to compare string-to-string in that
exact format. It works because the format is fixed-width and zero-padded,
so lexicographic string ordering happens to equal chronological ordering.
If the upstream data ever migrates to real BSON dates, `read_collection()`
would need to switch this filter to the `{"$date": iso}` form instead.

### The upsert — not a blind append

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant DF as Spark DataFrame (this run's batch)
    participant STG as staging table (_stg_&lt;collection&gt;)
    participant TGT as target table

    DF->>STG: write_staging() — JDBC "overwrite"<br/>(drop/recreate, scratch table)
    STG->>TGT: INSERT ... SELECT * FROM staging<br/>ON CONFLICT ("_id") DO UPDATE SET ...<br/>RETURNING (xmax = 0) AS inserted
    TGT-->>DF: rows_inserted / rows_updated<br/>split via the xmax=0 trick
    STG->>STG: DROP TABLE (cleanup, always runs — in a finally block)
```

Each document's Mongo `_id` is the merge key. New `_id`s insert; `_id`s
already present get updated in place — so field/status changes on
existing records are reflected correctly instead of creating duplicates
or being silently dropped. The `xmax = 0` trick is a Postgres-specific
way to tell, per returned row, whether the `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT`
actually inserted a new row or updated an existing one — `xmax` is a
system column that stays `0` for a freshly inserted row and gets set for
an updated one.

**Fallback ladder, not a hard requirement:** merging needs both an `_id`
column present *and* a unique index on it (created on demand via
`ensure_unique_id_index()`, which itself falls back gracefully — if
duplicate `_id`s already exist from some prior append-only run, the index
creation fails cleanly and the script logs a warning and continues in
append-only mode for that run, rather than crashing).

### Data type handling

`sanitize_for_postgres()` makes exactly two changes, both structurally
unavoidable over plain JDBC — nothing else about a column's type or
meaning is touched:

| Mongo type | Postgres representation |
|---|---|
| `_id` (BSON `ObjectId`) | cast to `string` — also doubles as the merge key |
| nested struct / array / map field | serialized to a JSON string via `F.to_json(...)` |

Every occurrence of a flattened field is logged (`complex_fields_flattened`
on the result, surfaced in the final Rich report), so it's always visible
when this happened, not a silent conversion.

### Control tables — plain Postgres, queryable with any client

Both live in the configured bronze schema, deliberately as ordinary
tables rather than anything extraction-specific:

| Table | Grain | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `etl_watermarks` | one row per collection (`table_name` is the PK) | current incremental state: which column, last value seen, last run's mode and insert/update counts |
| `etl_logs` | one row per collection **per run** | full audit trail: every field on `CollectionResult`, plus `run_id`, timing, and the full error text on failure |

`ensure_control_tables()` is fully idempotent — `CREATE TABLE IF NOT
EXISTS` plus a set of `ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS` migrations, safe to call
on every single run, including ones where these tables already exist
from an older version of the script with fewer columns.

### Validation — trust, but verify

After every write, `validate_collection()` independently re-counts the
target table and compares it against what the run *expected* to have
produced (`rows_before + rows_inserted` — updates don't change row
count) — deliberately not trusting whatever Spark or the merge query
itself reported. A mismatch sets `status = "VALIDATION FAILED"` and skips
updating the watermark for that collection, so a bad run doesn't get
treated as a clean checkpoint to resume from next time.

### Resilience: one bad collection doesn't stop the rest

`process_collection()`'s entire body is wrapped in one `try/except`, per
collection — a failure on `orders` is caught, logged, recorded to
`etl_logs`, and the loop moves on to the next collection rather than
aborting the whole run. `short_error()` collapses what can be a huge
multi-line Java/py4j/Python traceback down to one clean line for the
console and the summary table; the *full* traceback is still preserved
separately (`error_full`) and written to `etl_logs`, so nothing is
actually lost — it's just not dumped into the terminal.

### The version-compatibility guard — this is what `preflight` exists to prevent

```python
def check_connector_compatibility() -> None:
    major = int(pyspark.__version__.split(".")[0])
    if major >= 4:
        ...
        raise SystemExit(2)
```
`build_spark_session()` calls this before doing anything else. The pinned
`mongo-spark-connector` (`10.4.0`) is built against Spark 3.x's internals;
Spark 4.x changed enough that every read/write would fail with a cryptic
`java.lang.NoSuchMethodError`, repeated once per collection. This check
turns that into one clear message instead — but it's also, directly, the
exact failure mode the `preflight` stage in both `run_pipeline.ps1`
(`pipeline.md` §3) and the Airflow DAG (`airflow.md` §2) exists to head
off *before* `extract.py` ever runs, by pinning `pyspark==3.5.5` ahead of
time. This function is the safety net; `preflight` is what's meant to
make it never actually fire.

### Connector jars — cached locally, or resolved live

`build_spark_session()` checks whether all 5 jars the
`mongo-spark-connector` package would normally pull in via Maven/Ivy are
already present in `jars/`. If so, they're passed directly via
`spark.jars` and Ivy resolution is skipped entirely — no
`:: resolving dependencies ::` banner. If any are missing, it falls back
to `spark.jars.packages` (live Maven resolution) and prints a one-time
setup tip listing exactly which files to download and from where, so
subsequent runs can skip the network round trip.

### CLI arguments

| Flag | Effect |
|---|---|
| `--tables orders,customers` | Restrict the run to specific collections instead of auto-discovering all of them |
| `--full-refresh` | Ignore watermark state, truncate + fully reload every processed collection |
| `--dry-run` | Discover, count, and compute what *would* load — writes nothing |
| `--watermark-column <name>` | Force a specific column as the watermark for every collection, instead of auto-detecting per collection |

### Exit codes

| Code | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `0` | Every collection ended `OK` (or a harmless `SKIPPED`/`DRY-RUN`) |
| `1` | At least one collection's `status` is `FAILED` or `VALIDATION FAILED` — returned normally from `main()` |
| `2` | Unhandled `SQLAlchemyError` or any other uncaught exception at the very top level, before/outside the per-collection error handling |

Same contract every other pipeline stage relies on — a non-`0` exit here
is what `run_pipeline.ps1`'s `Stop-Pipeline` and the Airflow task's
default failure handling both key off.

### One inconsistency worth knowing about

`main()` gets its Mongo handle through the shared, cached
`get_mongo_db()` from `connection.py` (`utils.md` §3) — but builds its
own Postgres engine directly with a raw `create_engine(...)` call, rather
than calling `connection.py`'s equivalent `get_postgres_engine()`. Not
broken — it works — but it means this particular Postgres connection
skips `connection.py`'s round-trip ping check and caching, and won't
share a connection pool with anything else in the process that *did* go
through `get_postgres_engine()`.

---

## 2. `sql_test.py` — the runner behind every `*_sql_tests` stage

The generic engine underneath `tests/bronze`, `tests/silver`, and
`tests/gold`, documented from the pattern side in `tests.md`. This is the
actual implementation.

### Two auto-detected conventions, per file — not per folder

```python
"""
1. SELECT-style tests: query SELECTs the rows that VIOLATE the rule.
     Empty result set  -> PASS
     Any rows returned -> FAIL (rows written to the log file)

2. DO-block-style tests: the script raises its own exception when
   something is wrong.
     No exception raised -> PASS
     Exception raised    -> FAIL
"""
```

**Worth flagging:** the docstring's own examples list `tests/silver/*.sql`
and `tests/gold/*.sql` under the *SELECT-style* convention, and
`tests/bronze/*.sql` under *DO-block-style* — but the one silver file
actually verified in `tests.md` (`04_lp_check_negative_values.sql`) is
unambiguously a `DO $$ ... RAISE EXCEPTION` test, not a `SELECT`. The
code itself doesn't care which layer a file lives in — `run_tests()`
auto-detects the convention independently for every file it runs — so
this doesn't affect correctness, but the docstring's layer-to-convention
mapping doesn't match the one file confirmed so far. Treat "which
convention a given file uses" as something to check per file, not assume
from which folder it's in.

### Execution flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["sorted(test_dir.glob('*.sql'))"] --> B["for each file:<br/>conn.execute(text(query)), commit"]
    B --> C{"exception raised?<br/>(real DB error OR RAISE EXCEPTION)"}
    C -- yes --> D["rollback, log.error FAIL,<br/>continue to next file"]
    C -- no --> E{"result.fetchall() works?"}
    E -- no: ResourceClosedError --> F["DO block / DDL,<br/>no result set at all -> rows = []"]
    E -- yes --> G["rows = returned rows"]
    F --> H{"rows empty?"}
    G --> H
    H -- yes --> I["log.info: PASS"]
    H -- no --> J["log.error: FAIL (N failing rows)<br/>first 5 rows logged at DEBUG"]
```

The `ResourceClosedError` branch is the key piece that lets both
conventions share one code path: a `DO` block or plain DDL statement
genuinely has no result set to fetch at all — that's normal completion,
not a failure — versus a `SELECT` that legitimately returns zero rows,
which is also success but goes through the "empty result set" branch
instead. Both land on the same `PASS` outcome, via different routes.

**Rollback matters here specifically:** since every file in a directory
shares one open connection (`with engine.connect() as conn:` wraps the
whole loop), a failed statement — with or without an explicit `RAISE
EXCEPTION` — leaves the transaction in a failed state that would block
every subsequent file from running at all unless rolled back
immediately. That's why the exception branch calls `conn.rollback()`
before `continue`, rather than only logging the error.

### Exit codes

| Code | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `0` | Every `.sql` file in the directory passed |
| `1` | At least one file failed (either convention) |
| `2` | Usage error (wrong number of CLI args) or the target directory doesn't exist |

### Logging

`get_logger(logger_name, console_level=None)` — passing `console_level=None`
explicitly is functionally identical to omitting it (per `logger.py`'s
own `console_level if console_level is not None else level` fallback,
`utils.md` §4), landing back on the default `INFO`. Since `PASS` lines
are logged at `INFO` and `FAIL` at `ERROR`, both print to the console —
which is exactly the "one line per test" console behavior the module
docstring describes. The logger name itself is derived from the target
folder (`sql_tests_bronze`, `sql_tests_silver`, `sql_tests_gold`), so
each layer's run lands in its own dated log file.

---

## 3. How both scripts fit the rest of the pipeline

| | Stage in `run_pipeline.ps1` / DAG | Reads config via | Exit contract |
|---|---|---|---|
| `extract.py` | Stage 1: Extract | `utils/engine.py` (`utils.md` §2) | 0 / 1 / 2, see §1 |
| `sql_test.py` | Stages 2, 4, 6: `*_sql_tests` | `os.environ[...]` directly (not `utils/engine.py`) — expects `run_pipeline.ps1`/the DAG's `_PREAMBLE` to have already loaded `.env` | 0 / 1 / 2, see §2 |

Both rely on the same Postgres env vars (`POSTGRES_HOST`/`PORT`/
`DATABASE`/`USERNAME`/`PASSWORD`) being correct for wherever they're
running — `localhost` locally, `host.docker.internal` inside a
container, exactly as covered in `docker.md` §9 and `airflow.md` §5.

---

## 4. Quick reference

```bash
# extract.py
uv run python scripts/extract.py
uv run python scripts/extract.py --tables orders,customers
uv run python scripts/extract.py --full-refresh
uv run python scripts/extract.py --dry-run
uv run python scripts/extract.py --watermark-column updated_timestamp

# sql_test.py
uv run python scripts/sql_test.py tests/bronze
uv run python scripts/sql_test.py tests/silver
uv run python scripts/sql_test.py tests/gold
```